WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:05.420
- Growing up with the wrong information,

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.873
growing up with role
models as family members.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.440
In the ghetto, you know, in the hood,

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.520
we had this certain lingo that we spoke on

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.230
and everything that I was taught

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.817
from a early age was all bad.

00:00:20.817 --> 00:00:23.860
But in a sense it was good because

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:27.370
our conversations were about
getting over on the system.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.030
But I'm like, what system is that?

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.160
And the system was being
poor, black and in the ghetto.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.010
That was our prison kinda saying,

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.743
that was our training ground.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.200
And I remember

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:40.843
being 14, 15.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.700
You know, the older guys telling me,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.990
my older role models.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.575
Yeah, man, get in as much trouble
as you can before you turn

00:00:49.575 --> 00:00:52.580
18, 'cause once you turn 18,
you're gonna hit the county

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.960
and once you get there, man, it's just

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.580
a whole 'nother level.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.820
Now, they painted this picture of prison

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.400
being like Camp Snoopy, you
know, we had the weights,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.030
they had the TV, they had
cigarettes, they had coffee.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.740
So to me, it was like going on adventure,

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.130
say going to the amusement
park, like a Magic Mountain

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.580
or an Oxbury Farm, that's
the way I depicted prison

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.780
back then even though I'd
never been there because

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.740
it was the picture that was painted to me.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:21.620
Made it so alluring, it's
like, oh, man, I can't wait

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.967
to experience that and...

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.590
Not knowing that

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.340
it'd be one of those kind of...

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.130
Home away from homes that
just, completely, just engulfed

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:36.337
me, man and it kinda had me
messed up in more ways than one.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.120
First time I went to prison,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.000
I had a lot of stuff going on, you know,

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.610
in my life at the time
and I needed a break.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:50.560
So, I think I was sentenced to 16 months.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.800
16 with half, for strong armed robbery.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.680
And I did 10 months and a couple weeks.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.210
But they had sent me to Fire
Camp and I was fighting fires

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:04.780
and what an adventure that
was, man, being in a cabin,

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.270
being in the mountains,
you know, away from society

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.620
and no barbed wire fence, no guard towers

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.230
or nothin' like that.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.440
Eatin' like a king and
being on the front line

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.030
fighting fires, that was
a hell of an adventure

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.283
and that was the first
impression I got from prison.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.120
That was it, you know,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.180
I came in looking like Alfalfa

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:27.920
and I came home looking like The Rock.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.740
In 10 months, man.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.640
That transformation just took
place and I was like, oh man,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.123
it's everything they said
it was plus more, prison.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.940
You know but I didn't
even know at the time

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.040
and I was in prison in my own mind,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.910
body and spirit, prior to going there.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.810
And the cold thing about it
is I was looking at that,

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.520
looking at that part
as a release, you know,

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.040
and everything, things were
hard on the streets and I

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:55.160
couldn't maintain, I couldn't
handle, I knew where to go

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.700
to find that release to get
that pressure up off of me,

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.550
you know, dealing with the things

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.460
that were going on in the 80s.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.750
You know, the 80s were the
roughest part of my life.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.130
You know, I can't stand the 80s.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.880
What I went through, what I
experienced and being exposed

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.473
to in the 80s, you know, with everything

00:03:12.473 --> 00:03:14.873
that was going on in the
neighborhood back then.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:16.713
And

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:19.080
the 80s.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.890
Man, I couldn't wait to escape, man.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:24.920
And I found a way out, you know?

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.570
I do know it was the
wrong way, but yet, still,

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.650
I found a way out from
dealing with that bullshit

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.440
family situation, from dealing
with the crack epidemic

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.640
and from dealing with that
gang stuff that was going on.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:37.400
Man, it was all these different
things going on in my life

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.272
at the time and I just didn't
know how to deal with it.

00:03:40.272 --> 00:03:42.600
For the life of me, I just couldn't.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:43.740
I couldn't fathom

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.130
growing up in a predicament that I was in.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.483
And just saw myself
drowning in quick sand.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.010
You know, at the same
time and looking around

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.070
and couldn't see no help.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.450
There was no help for me
back then and I knew it.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.423
Shit, I knew I was in deep trouble.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.350
And that shit kinda hurt me.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.500
And so...

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:08.750
My second trip to prison,

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.200
I did another strong armed robbery

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.720
and they gave me four years.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.973
Once again, I went to Fire Camp, so.

00:04:15.937 --> 00:04:18.720
I go through the PFT
and all that kinda stuff

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.220
and I excelled at the
exercise, to the jumping jacks

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.440
to running the mile and
the pull ups and the dips

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.630
and all that and mind
you, they had weights.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.760
And once again, the second time

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:30.970
I turned up in prison, you know,

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.540
I was looking like Alfalfa.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.660
You know, scrawny,
skinny, 155 soaking wet.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.090
And I think I did 27 months on that joke.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:44.213
And I came out like
220, fuckin' huge, man.

00:04:44.213 --> 00:04:47.420
Lookin' like a action
hero, for real, you know?

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.515
'Cause I didn't even look real to myself.

00:04:49.515 --> 00:04:50.950
And then when I used to
see, encounter people

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:52.787
on the streets like my family, they said,

00:04:52.787 --> 00:04:56.420
"My God, Eric, you know,
man, what happened?"

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.800
Shit, I don't know, your
guess as good as mine.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.700
I guess it was that good
eatin', fightin' the fires,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.520
being in the best shape of my life.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.240
Then my 20s, as I was going through that

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.100
experience with the prison,

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:07.540
now, mind you,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.770
my first impression was
Fire Camp, eating good,

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.170
making good money and not
being around no guards

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.980
and all that kinda stuff,
no walls, no barbed wire.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:18.440
But as I kept going back
and forth, it started

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.483
getting worser and worser and worser.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:27.290
And I think my third time they
didn't send me to Fire Camp

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.740
because my points had rose

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:33.320
because of the two prior
trips of going to prison.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.110
Now, I have five prior commitments

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:38.210
and I have about

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.280
15, let's call it 15

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:45.470
parole violations.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:49.000
You know, one year,
seven months, this, that.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.870
And but on my third time,
man, I didn't go to Fire Camp.

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:54.340
They said I didn't qualify for it because

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.310
my case was a serious violent.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.600
So back in this time they had
the three strikes going on.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.220
They had all these laws coming up

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:03.440
and things were slowly
changing in the system, man.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:08.050
And I didn't really notice
it until it was too late.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:10.210
But by the time I came
to the realization about

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.300
the changes that was
going on in prison, man,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.740
I was already lost in the sauce.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.350
You know, and what I mean by that

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:18.730
is they start taking things away.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:21.360
They start making it where it wasn't fun

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.110
no more and wantin' to go to prison.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:25.810
You know, I mean, I got
to the level three yard.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.180
It was way different than Fire Camp.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:31.120
You had, I was in a cell,
living with a celly.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.910
They had the barbed wire, the gun towers

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.020
and no warning shots
and all that bullshit,

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:38.950
so that was like a culture shock to me.

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:42.820
Because I was used to
Camp Snoopy, Disneyland.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:45.470
And my reality was far from that.

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:46.630
It wasn't no more Disneyland,

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.820
there wasn't no more Camp Snoopy.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:50.710
You know, it was where you
really had to be on your

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:54.066
Ps and Qs, man, it wasn't no
guarantee you'll make it home.

00:06:54.066 --> 00:06:56.993
And I was like, goddamn,
you got to be kidding me.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:01.190
Ain't no, no, there they
ain't no guarantee, not here.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:03.280
You know, and for the first time

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:05.840
I got to see the difference in how

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.870
the longer people stay in
prison, say the lifers,

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:13.510
they come home, you know,
they got more stuff going on.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:15.640
Now for me, I think when
I went there, I probably

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.400
had maybe three years, they sentenced me

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.330
to three years for a gun
that didn't belong to me.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:24.460
And I think I was home for two days only.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:26.960
And I went back, two days on
the streets, went right back

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.360
and that's when they sent
me to that level three

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:30.150
for the first time.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:34.033
And I never knew anything about PTSD.

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.270
But I understand being
in prison, it's that

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:39.330
kinda thing that goes on in there.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:42.540
Now, while you in there you got
to stay on top of your game.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:44.370
You got to use your A game.

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.420
You got to stay with your race.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.050
They got the prison politics bullshit

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.410
going up in there so you got to play that

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:52.620
and most importantly, you
got to stay out of trouble.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.930
You know, you've got to
learn how to do your program

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.160
but stay out of the way of
others and that's not easy

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:00.680
because there's always something
that's liable to happen

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.770
in prison beyond your
control, like the riots,

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:09.100
lock downs, modified programs
and so it was dangerous.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.920
Now, I remember, say my four, fifth

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.243
trip going back and forth,

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:17.600
it didn't dawn on me that
I experienced something

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.960
worse than being on the streets
with my bout with the crack,

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:22.840
with the environment and
everything that came with that.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:25.330
Now, that was a heavy burden
to carry on the streets

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:28.130
because being addicted ain't no joke.

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:31.740
And so I remember coming from prison,

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.680
coming home, I don't give
a damn how long I was gone

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.540
and then ended up in the same spot where

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.730
I left out at fuckin' with that crack.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.040
I'm like, how the fuck I get here?

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:42.380
Because in that other place it was worser

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:43.743
but in a different way.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:46.670
You know, I had to...

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:51.510
It's like going to the military bootcamp.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:55.000
I had to gear up and be
prepared, mind, body and spirit

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.850
for this prison war kinda
setting, not the jungle war

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:02.190
but the prison, the concrete
war, the concrete jungle.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.680
And now, that's what prison was, man.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.520
You know, you had these races up in there

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.670
and you know, everybody
stays separate and all that

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.857
bullshit and you know, they
had everything set up, man.

00:09:11.857 --> 00:09:14.880
And you know, we had our own
separate tables and, you know,

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:17.180
it's like going back in
the motherfuckin' 60s, man.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:20.177
Where we had the colored
drinking fountain and the white.

00:09:20.177 --> 00:09:21.160
That's what it feel like in prison.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:22.660
Mexicans sat at they motherfuckin' table.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.120
We had our two or three,
the whites had theirs.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.810
I'm like, man, you know, we
can interact with each other.

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:31.190
But you know, there was
lines that you couldn't cross

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:33.540
in there, you know, or
there was some consequences.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:35.380
So, you know,

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:37.400
the more I was goin', the more I got wise

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:41.480
to the intertwinings
of the prison setting.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:44.940
And most importantly, the
most important factor, man,

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:49.560
that I missed was not seeing
it as a plantation, man,

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:50.839
as the...

00:09:50.839 --> 00:09:53.672
(Eric stammering)

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:58.520
I started in '87, you know,
going to prison and everything.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:02.060
But I never looked at it as
going to a slave plantation

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.780
until later on, man, because
the more I kept going back

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:06.300
and forth, the more I
started reading books,

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.780
making inquiries and really
taking a look around,

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:11.660
they have 34 prisons in California alone

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.210
and a majority of 'em
set up of men of color.

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:16.250
And that's the God's honest
truth and if I didn't go

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.360
so many motherfuckin'
times, I wouldn't know that.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.270
But my experience tells
me that 'cause I done been

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.423
to about 15 different
prisons in California alone.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:26.603
Doing my 19 trips.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:29.210
You know, it was fun at the beginning

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:31.710
but in the end, it wasn't fun no more.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:33.850
Wasn't nothing funny
about going to prison,

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.570
not when reality set in.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.280
Shit, but I was already,
it was too late for me.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.130
I was already entangled in that shit.

00:10:40.028 --> 00:10:42.273
And for the life of me, I
couldn't find my way out of it.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.600
And so what happened was, the more

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.750
I was going back and forth and seeing

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:53.343
that it wasn't all cracked up
what it was supposed to be.

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:57.053
It still didn't dawn on me,
man, because I was always

00:10:57.053 --> 00:11:00.330
looking at prison as
my home away from home,

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:02.820
which in turn, became my home from home.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.720
Because I did more time in
prison than I did on the streets

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:07.543
during my 20s and my 30s.

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:13.290
And I think the last time
I went in, I went in at 43

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:17.670
and I came out at 50, I'm 53 right now.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:20.690
But my last trip I did
about six and a half,

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:22.550
close to seven years, man.

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:26.170
And that was my last bout with visiting,

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:29.680
you know, my home away from home here.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.260
And well, I'd just like
to share something about

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:35.393
the prison setting, man,
and how it was set up.

00:11:36.869 --> 00:11:40.740
And so I'm looking at it as
a way to escape my reality

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:45.740
on the streets but it was at
the point where, I think in...

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:49.920
They saw where we were benefiting

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.390
from the coffee, from the
cigarettes, from the weights

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:54.420
and the guards were getting
scared of us because

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.550
we would go in scrawny and come out

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:59.160
like the Incredible Hulk.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:00.930
You know, and I paid witness
to that, man, I think

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:02.640
on my trip when I did four with half, man,

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:07.200
I came out so huge, I looked
like Arnold in his prime.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.510
Fuckin' cut, man, ripped, arms swole.

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:13.440
Blew my mind and that's
what I used to look forward

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.450
to by going to prison, was
those kinda experiences.

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.800
Let aside the race riots
and all that other bullshit.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.197
My whole thing was if I end up in prison,

00:12:22.197 --> 00:12:23.230
I know what Ima do.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:25.707
Ima abide by the rules and the guidelines.

00:12:25.707 --> 00:12:28.060
Ima be the model motherfuckin'
inmate and I'm gonna get on

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:30.870
swole and try this bullshit
on the streets again.

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:33.220
Not knowing that I was
bringing some of the bullshit

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:36.163
from the prison setting out
on the streets were compacted.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.360
I couldn't succeed on the
streets because I took the shit

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.350
from the streets that I endured
to get to prison thinking

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:45.130
I'm escaping and just added
some more stuff to the equation

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:46.920
that had nothing to do with
the streets but the prison,

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:49.330
kinda, bylaws and guidelines and I brought

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:51.393
that shit to it and it just didn't work.

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:53.113
It didn't work.

00:12:53.113 --> 00:12:54.173
So what I learned on the streets,

00:12:54.173 --> 00:12:56.680
that shit don't work in prison.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:57.513
And what I learned in prison,

00:12:57.513 --> 00:12:59.970
it doesn't work on the streets.

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:03.010
It's two different settings,
man, it's two different.

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:04.460
I didn't know it at the time.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:06.770
So what happened was...

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:09.783
I got caught up in the...

00:13:11.207 --> 00:13:13.560
And the just knowing that

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:16.513
once I got to prison, I know
it wasn't gonna be all good.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:20.160
By four but I knew it wasn't
gonna be all bad either.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:24.320
So in 1987, excuse me, 1990,

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:27.180
I started in 87, so 10 years into it.

00:13:28.215 --> 00:13:31.343
1997, they took the weights off the yard.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:34.740
They went from taking all the
heavy stuff and leaving us

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:37.960
so much, like they say
they take the 150 dumbbells

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:40.520
down to the 100 to the 95s and the 80s.

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:42.978
They leave us the 75s on down.

00:13:42.978 --> 00:13:44.700
They take all the extra
quarters and they rolled

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:48.060
a 50 and a 25, 75 only for the bench.

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.080
So they took the majority
of weights and left us

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:53.550
very little to work with
because they said we were just

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:57.199
coming home too big and
the guards were, the guards

00:13:57.199 --> 00:13:58.420
were more, they weren't in shape.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:13:59.530
They weren't at the top of they game.

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:01.270
You know, being a prison guard, you just

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:02.950
go there to do your thing and go home.

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:04.810
So they wasn't tripping
on trying to be in shape.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:08.150
A few were health conscious,
but the majority of the prison

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:11.221
guards there, they're just
there to do the job, man

00:14:11.221 --> 00:14:13.871
and goin' home, collect they
pay, I'm not mad at 'em.

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:20.070
So they took the weights.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.510
They took the majority of
the heavy, the extra stuff

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:24.790
and right behind that, they took

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:26.353
all the weights off the yard.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:29.270
Right up under our noses,
they just came one day

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:33.530
with some trucks and they
had these inmates come, man,

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:36.570
and they just took
everything off the yard, man.

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:39.820
It took them maybe two days

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:41.770
and they had us on modified program.

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:44.280
You know, we just went and
eat and went back to our dorms

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.620
because they didn't want
us around that while

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:48.950
it was taking place, man,
until they were done.

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:51.090
And it took two days for
them to take everything off

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:53.430
the yard, I didn't see it
at the time as a deterrent

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:55.050
to keep people from wanting
to come back and forth.

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:57.150
I couldn't see it back
then but they was trying

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:59.140
to come up with different
methods so yo ass

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:00.610
wouldn't wanna come to prison no more.

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:02.280
They didn't wanna make it
easy, wanna make it look

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.600
like Camp Snoopy which it was back then.

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:06.080
They said, well, you know,

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:07.720
guys don't mind coming in
and out, they don't mind

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:11.030
coming back and forth because
of the set up, the layout.

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:13.213
And so they started with the weights.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:17.240
Now, what's most important
in prison is weights,

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:18.593
coffee, and tobacco.

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:21.000
Guess what was next on the list?

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:21.833
I think,

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:24.576
think shortly after that, they took

00:15:24.576 --> 00:15:26.780
the tobacco off the yard.

00:15:26.780 --> 00:15:27.613
And...

00:15:28.630 --> 00:15:29.463
I say...

00:15:31.010 --> 00:15:34.010
85% of men in prison smoke
cigarettes, drink coffee

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.680
and work out, 85% out of 100.

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:40.830
That's a lot, so the prison
is making a killing on tobacco

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:43.300
and coffee, of course.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.750
And like I said, when they had
the weights and everything,

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.720
the workout kinda thing
going on, 'cause everybody

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:49.670
in prison was health and
fitness, even if yo ass

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:51.680
didn't want to, you had
to get yo ass in line

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:53.010
just in case some shit jumped off.

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:54.730
You'd be, you know, you'd be a little bit

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:56.310
in a better position to
take care of yourself.

00:15:56.310 --> 00:15:59.000
So all races, it was
mandatory that you work out.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:01.480
I love working out, so it
wasn't mandatory for me.

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:03.070
Shit, when I went to prison, now, you

00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:04.410
couldn't keep me off the weights.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:07.040
You know, that was my thing, you know?

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:10.760
And, but for some people,
it wasn't their thing

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:13.050
but they had no choice
because like I said,

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:16.050
what applies in there doesn't
apply out here and vice versa.

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:18.750
After they took

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:22.150
the tobacco,

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:24.430
you know, people was in
a uproar, man, because,

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:25.960
like I said, that's one of the number one

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:29.460
money factors in prison, man, is tobacco.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:31.970
And once they took that, man,

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:33.850
it still didn't dawn on me

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:37.110
that they were trying to make
it hard instead of easy, man.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:39.480
You know, they were trying to deter me

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.283
but I couldn't see it back
then, man, 'cause like I said,

00:16:41.283 --> 00:16:43.560
I was lost in the politics of the shit.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.594
And I had my own troubles in there

00:16:45.594 --> 00:16:46.770
and I had my own troubles in the streets.

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:48.890
So it was like, damn, I dread,

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:50.330
when it's time for me
to parole and go home,

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:52.810
I dreaded going home, man, and then when

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:55.420
I did my bullshit on the
streets and got caught,

00:16:55.420 --> 00:16:57.170
it's like, God, and to some degree

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:58.991
I dreaded going to prison.

00:16:58.991 --> 00:17:00.830
Because of the bullshit
that came with it, you know,

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:02.690
and plus they took the
weights off the yard,

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:05.373
they took the tobacco
and I love, I used to...

00:17:05.373 --> 00:17:08.540
Back in prison, they had
a Bugler rolling tobacco

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:10.390
and I was a Bugler
connoisseur, man, I'd roll

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:13.060
the hell out some Bugler cigarettes.

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:14.750
You know, I mastered it,
you know, I could roll one

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:17.380
behind my back, man, with one hand.

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:19.970
You know, and, shit, I took
my show on the road, man.

00:17:19.970 --> 00:17:22.500
I took it to the streets,
man, and I was on the streets

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.070
goin' to the tobacco shop
back in the early 90s, man,

00:17:25.070 --> 00:17:27.470
buying a can of Bugler
for like 20 bucks, man,

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:29.400
and coming with 200 papers.

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:31.840
And I'm rolling my ass off, man
and walking down the street.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:35.690
I remember some LAPD officers

00:17:35.690 --> 00:17:38.640
driving by lookin', 'cause it
looked like marijuana sticks

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:40.510
but it was just rolling tobacco.

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:42.580
But I rolled 'em like, like I said, like,

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:45.200
they look like joints.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:48.283
You know, and I was on Bugler, man.

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:51.500
Even after they weren't
popular no more on the streets,

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:53.600
you know, 'cause people
started going to like regular

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:55.580
cigarettes, so I went from the Buglers

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:57.800
to the fuckin' Camel non-filters.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:17:59.080
You know, 'cause that was another thing.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:02.210
This mantra about being
in prison, you know,

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:04.460
everybody, you knew who was in prison

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:06.040
from what they smoked on the streets.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:08.250
So if you see somebody
with a pouch of Bugler,

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:09.590
you knew that motherfucker was in prison.

00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:11.530
If you see somebody with a
pack of Camel non-filters,

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:13.970
you knew he was from prison,
too, but it seemed like

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:16.840
in a higher levels, they was
doing a Camel non-filters.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:18.070
In the Fire Camp, stuff like that,

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:19.820
they was doing the rolling tobacco.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:21.553
And...

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:24.903
So I think my,

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:27.820
my seventh, eighth trip,

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:29.760
it started getting worse
and worse in there, man.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:31.500
They start coming up with
all these guidelines,

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:33.560
all these rules and
all that bullshit, man,

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.340
They made it hard on you.

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:37.333
And I still couldn't see it.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:41.060
You know, I still couldn't see
that they was really trying

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:44.450
to get a hold on people not
wanting to come back and forth

00:18:44.450 --> 00:18:47.920
to prison, man, and in the
beginning, for me, man,

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:52.200
it was, you know, it was like
playing a cat and mouse game.

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:53.033
You know?

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.870
What I can get away with
while I'm in there, you know,

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.750
how can I out slick,
you know, the counselors

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:02.570
and the guards and all that kinda shit

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:04.570
by being this model inmate.

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:06.770
But really putting forth an effort, man.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:08.580
But just not having
nothing to work with, man,

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:11.110
because they didn't give you
nothing viable in prison.

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:13.840
You know, I just knew what I
had comin' when I got there.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:16.920
I knew what time count was, I
knew what jobs they kinda gave

00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:20.050
out, you know, you doin'
poor, the kitchen worker.

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:21.990
Shit like that, education

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:24.320
and special services,
you know, that was it.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:26.230
That's all, they, it was limited, what you

00:19:26.230 --> 00:19:28.450
could do in prison, as
far as work, so I already

00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:30.830
knew going in what I was gonna do, man.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:32.710
Man, put me in the kitchen.

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:35.010
Yeah, put my ass right there in the back,

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.340
doing the pots and pans
so I can be out the way

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:39.440
and I could steal everything
I got my hands on, man.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:41.700
And sell to back to the
fellas when I get there.

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:44.760
The bell peppers, the onions,
the mustard, whatever, man.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:46.260
You know, I was in there surviving, man,

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:47.800
trying to survive, man.

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:50.020
'Cause it's hard in prison
when you're in there

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:51.740
by yourself and you don't have no support.

00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:55.080
It's hard as hell and a lot of
people couldn't take it, man.

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:55.913
And they sunk.

00:19:59.146 --> 00:20:03.000
So they get up into the black
market kinda thing in prison.

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:06.480
Cell phones, tobacco, drugs.

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:08.713
I didn't play that shit, I'm
like, man, fuck that shit.

00:20:08.713 --> 00:20:10.930
I wanna go home when
it's my turn to go home

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:12.880
because everybody get a turn to go home.

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:15.600
I just didn't know all those
trips leading up to my 19th

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:19.110
that I didn't have a plan and
I wasn't prepared for home.

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:21.260
So even though I was a model
inmate, even though I did all

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:23.430
the self help classes, even
though I came in one way,

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:24.820
came out a different way,

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:26.870
I was still working with
the same information.

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:28.770
I was still working with
the same thing emotionally.

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:31.490
I was still working with
the same kid, mentally.

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:34.360
You know, I was just getting
older in age, you know,

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:36.260
as the trips came on, as you know,

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:39.630
from my first trip to like
the 15th trip, I was 18.

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:42.817
Then I found myself at 35 by my 18th trip.

00:20:42.817 --> 00:20:45.860
And from 18 to 35, I was still
working with the same old

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:48.733
feelings, man, didn't
nothin' change from 18 to 35.

00:20:48.733 --> 00:20:51.450
I don't give a fuck if I
went 1,000 times to prison.

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:52.800
Didn't nothin' change, man.

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:58.830
I was still working
with that same mindset.

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:01.640
Prison didn't have no psychiatry,
didn't have no counseling,

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:03.830
they didn't have all that,
the one on ones in there, man,

00:21:03.830 --> 00:21:06.180
you was on your own
motherfuckin' own up in there.

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:09.020
And either your ass was gonna
swim or you was gonna sink.

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:11.430
And by, meaning sink,
you was gonna get yo ass

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:15.183
rolled up to the SNY yards
and all that kinda stuff, man.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:18.743
All my 19 trips, I went
to general population.

00:21:21.174 --> 00:21:23.280
I never put myself in a position
where I had to get rolled

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:25.840
up and moved off the yard,
man, because I didn't play that

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:28.200
shit, I knew what I had
to do and I did it well

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:31.480
up in prison, I just wish I
woulda had just as much success

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:34.740
as I had in prison by
walking that fine line,

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:35.833
like on the streets.

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:38.830
I couldn't walk that fine
line on the streets like

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:41.570
I did in there but I had
the guards hoverin' over me.

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:43.300
I had the inmates hoverin'
over me to make sure

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:46.710
that I didn't falter,
have a misstep because

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:50.253
in there, you have a
misstep, that's yo ass.

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:52.970
I'm not playing.

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.260
You talk about some shit
going on up in there, man,

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:57.110
by brothers not paying attention

00:21:57.110 --> 00:22:00.430
and following the rules and
guidelines in there, man.

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:03.113
The consequences are
deep and they're heavy.

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:05.223
And...

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:10.713
So it took me, I think my
19th trip, man, to prison,

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:13.890
where I finally got it.

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:16.599
So what happened was nothin' changed from

00:22:16.599 --> 00:22:20.080
the routine that I had
established from my first time

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:22.220
being in Fire Camp, I
still worked out every

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:24.530
motherfuckin' day, I still
did all the self-help classes.

00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:26.370
I still did the church
and all that kinda stuff

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:29.343
and I still stayed out the
way, man, I stayed in my lane.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:33.330
You know, and that didn't
change because it worked.

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:35.240
Now, I didn't have no
success on the street

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:36.680
with it but it worked in there, so, shit,

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:38.280
if it ain't broke, don't fix it.

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:40.113
And...

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:43.293
What happened was, man,

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:45.873
this time

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:48.650
when I paroled,

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:51.050
things started to click for me.

00:22:51.050 --> 00:22:53.270
I kinda see where

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:55.890
the way the prison is set
up, it's set up for you,

00:22:55.890 --> 00:22:59.910
not to succeed but to fail
because prison is what you

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:01.510
put into it is what you get up out of it.

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:03.368
If you don't put nothin' into it, then

00:23:03.368 --> 00:23:04.700
yo ass ain't got nothin' comin'.

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:08.563
So having been sentenced to nine years,

00:23:09.510 --> 00:23:11.500
waiting to present, it wasn't
just no in and out, back

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:14.530
and forth like I been, you
know, going up to the 18 times,

00:23:14.530 --> 00:23:17.640
seven months, nine months,
three months, four months,

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:21.480
five months, two years, three
years, 60 months, four years.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.000
It wasn't that in and out, back
and forth kinda shit, like,

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:25.720
now you see me, now you don't.

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:28.190
When they gave me that nine years with 80,

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:31.140
I really had to sit my ass down

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:33.730
and really take a look at
what was going on around me

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:36.780
all the time that I didn't
see the first 18 times.

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:38.740
I saw a lot of men come
in there, didn't put

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:41.460
a motherfuckin' thing into it
and when it's time for them

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:44.323
to get they turn to take they
ass home, scared to death.

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:47.300
'Cause they didn't have no
plan, they wasn't prepared

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:49.140
and I remember getting up in the morning

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:50.850
'cause they used to release us,

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:52.760
in prison, after breakfast sometimes.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:55.500
So you have to be in R&amp;R,
like, by 9:00 in the morning.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:57.470
So they give you your
little parole clothes,

00:23:57.470 --> 00:23:59.320
all that bullshit and
take you to the train,

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:01.410
the bus station, give you a little $200

00:24:01.410 --> 00:24:05.263
and that's it, see ya, bon
voyage, good luck, godspeed.

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:09.130
And I remember being in a
TV room or the game room

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:11.530
or wherever the fuck I
happened to be at that time

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:15.220
and I see these men, you
know, being called to go home

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:18.720
and I used to watch 'em and
some of 'em be so scared

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:22.220
and so I could see the
despair written over they face

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:23.970
because they really didn't
do a motherfuckin' thing

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:26.170
while I was there, man, they
just went through the motions.

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:29.030
I went through the motions a
lot of times in the beginning.

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:30.850
Think the first 15
motherfuckin' trips for me,

00:24:30.850 --> 00:24:32.780
I went through the motions.

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:34.690
Then I wonder why I didn't stand a chance

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:35.943
out here on the streets.

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:38.103
Shit.

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:42.240
If you want it, you got to
get it and I didn't see that

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:43.980
at the time, I know
how to work, I knew how

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:47.230
to get it when it came to
weight training, I love it.

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:49.290
I know how to get that, I
know everything, in and out

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:51.953
about training, man, about bodybuilding.

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:55.820
You know, shit, that's me.

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:56.653
Love it!

00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:03.910
But the last trip I was just watching

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:06.957
and the more I paid
attention, the more things

00:25:06.957 --> 00:25:09.330
started to click for me and
it came together for me.

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:12.530
And I saw how prison is not
designed to help yo ass.

00:25:12.530 --> 00:25:15.420
It's not built to help
you succeed when you

00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:18.210
get yo motherfuckin'
ass back on the streets.

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:20.770
Prison is there for you to
pay your debt to society,

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:23.120
for whatever the fuck you
did prior, on the streets

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:25.100
that got you in a judical
court system, that

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:27.390
got you sentenced to a place like that.

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:30.580
It's not there to do the
pep talks to help you.

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:34.310
Man, you got to pay yo
debt, whatever crime it is.

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:37.460
You know, so, it's not set up,

00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:41.420
it's not designed as a
good place to wanna go.

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:43.820
You know, it's a place that,

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:46.080
even more so now, before I paroled,

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:49.250
this last time, man, they
took, they took so much stuff

00:25:49.250 --> 00:25:51.533
and they changed the
rules so much, I vowed,

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:54.940
'cause it started, like I
said, I started really watching

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:57.150
and paying attention because
I was there for a long time.

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:59.810
It just wasn't no, in and
out, hop, skip and a jump.

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:02.410
I really sat, they sat
my ass down long enough

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:04.630
for me to really see what
was going on around me

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:07.105
and why it was so hard
for me to stay outta there

00:26:07.105 --> 00:26:08.400
and why it was so hard
for me to stay successful

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:11.463
on the streets because I tried
to combine the two together

00:26:11.463 --> 00:26:14.200
and they just don't mix,
it's like oil and water.

00:26:14.200 --> 00:26:17.030
And I always tried to take
some of the good stuff from

00:26:17.030 --> 00:26:20.077
prison and apply it on the
streets, but it never worked.

00:26:20.077 --> 00:26:22.130
And I tried to take some of
the good morals and values

00:26:22.130 --> 00:26:24.070
I was learning, take that shit,
man, they don't give a fuck

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:27.113
about that, how you was
raised and how good you are.

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:36.070
So I tried to be the model

00:26:36.070 --> 00:26:38.630
inmate in prison but
they don't give a fuck

00:26:38.630 --> 00:26:40.420
about no model inmate.

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:44.830
And I tried to be the model
citizen on the streets where

00:26:44.830 --> 00:26:47.700
most characters, most cats,
man, they don't give a fuck

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:50.940
about no model, productive citizen

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:53.160
in a motherfuckin' crack house.

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:55.910
In the hood, they can
care less about that.

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:57.260
You better not be talking about what you

00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:58.660
learned and what you gained.

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:01.780
Liable to get your throat cut,
'cause ain't nobody on that

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:04.570
page, ain't nobody trying
to hear that good shit.

00:27:04.570 --> 00:27:06.510
Not from where I come from.

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:08.230
You better be about the hustle,

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:10.440
about the paper, about the money.

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:13.710
'Cause if it don't make
dollars, it don't make sense.

00:27:13.710 --> 00:27:14.543
And...

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:17.330
Yeah, I was convinced of that one time,

00:27:17.330 --> 00:27:19.920
but not no more, man, you know?

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:22.670
So what I did, man, my last
trip, man, I did what made my

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:25.120
motherfuckin' ass happy and
that was just to pay attention

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:28.130
and watch all the comings and
goings and kept my mouth shut

00:27:28.130 --> 00:27:31.665
and did more observing, then I
realized why it never worked.

00:27:31.665 --> 00:27:33.780
I realized now why
things never panned out,

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:35.690
why things never worked out.

00:27:35.690 --> 00:27:38.280
Because I was applying the wrong methods

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:40.500
to the streets and vice versa.

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:43.993
So I got hooked up with the right people.

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:46.680
I took advantage of the
teachers, the instructors

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:49.100
that was there, I got all the information

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:51.930
I could and it was back and forth.

00:27:51.930 --> 00:27:54.420
And when I paroled...

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:55.830
January, February, March, April, May.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:59.330
I paroled May 17th, 2017.

00:27:59.330 --> 00:28:01.630
Man, I ain't look back,
I ain't missed a beat.

00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:06.250
Because for the first time
in my life, man, out of all

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:10.020
those motherfuckin' trips
to prison, I had a plan

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:13.660
and I was prepared, but you know what?

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:15.170
It might've took,

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:17.730
you know, those 18 times
prior just to get me

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:20.100
to that place where I
said, enough is enough

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:22.620
and I better do something
about my dire situation

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:25.170
because in the beginning
it wasn't a dire situation

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.757
until I got, until I started at 18

00:28:27.757 --> 00:28:29.970
and then I went in at 43.

00:28:29.970 --> 00:28:32.650
So when I was at 43, with that nine years,

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:36.170
I'm like, goddamn, man, it ain't no game,

00:28:36.170 --> 00:28:38.761
it ain't no joke up in here, man.

00:28:38.761 --> 00:28:39.980
This shit ain't no game,
it ain't no guarantee

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:42.480
I'm going home, I got the
PTSD, the longer yo ass stay

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:45.370
up in there, you gonna come
home with some kind of problems.

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:48.910
Ask a lifer that do 26 or 38
years and get his ass home.

00:28:48.910 --> 00:28:50.730
It's gonna take him a
long time to get that shit

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:53.550
up out of his system, whatever it is.

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:56.070
And me doing six and a half years,

00:28:56.070 --> 00:28:58.310
man, some of that stuff
is still seeping through

00:28:58.310 --> 00:29:01.210
my pores, man, still running through me.

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:03.520
You know, I didn't know,

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:06.100
back and forth, in and out
that it just accumulated,

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:07.990
accumulated, accumulated, accumulated

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:09.270
and I been holding on to the stuff

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:11.210
for as long as I can remember.

00:29:11.210 --> 00:29:16.130
Now, I'm finally able to
release it, but it's not easy.

00:29:16.130 --> 00:29:19.163
It'll never be easy because I'll notice,

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:22.360
all the shit I been through in prison,

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:24.580
with not knowin', you know,
I been in some riots, man.

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:26.750
I been in with the fights and all that

00:29:26.750 --> 00:29:28.890
and being close to knives
and all that bullshit

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:31.660
and the gunshots, I been
through all of that.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:34.060
But for the life of me, when
I came home, I couldn't talk,

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:36.390
I didn't have nobody to
talk to about that stuff

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:38.350
I experienced in prison,
I don't give a fuck

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:40.180
if I went 1,000 times.

00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:42.190
My 19 trips to prison and
every time I came home,

00:29:42.190 --> 00:29:45.690
I didn't talk about that, nobody
wanted to hear about that.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:47.560
So I kept that shit
bottled up inside of me,

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.080
all my experiences in prison.

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:52.030
So I didn't have no release.

00:29:52.030 --> 00:29:53.850
So I'm walking around with
all that bullshit on my

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:56.060
shoulders, then I come on
the streets and I got to deal

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:58.430
with the family situation
and family dynamics,

00:29:58.430 --> 00:30:00.470
not having a personal
relationships with nobody,

00:30:00.470 --> 00:30:02.610
dealing with the crack
cocaine bullshit episode

00:30:02.610 --> 00:30:05.540
and all that, so it was
just, I was just like

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:06.723
a walking time bomb.

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:12.410
And it took me a long time,
man, to see it for what it was.

00:30:12.410 --> 00:30:15.190
You know, that's why it is
so imperative for me to talk

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:18.346
about this stuff, man,
because I've been holding

00:30:18.346 --> 00:30:20.146
on to it for so long, it'll kill me.

00:30:20.998 --> 00:30:22.310
And if I don't talk about
it, I'll have my ass

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:24.423
back in prison, it's inevitable.

00:30:25.529 --> 00:30:27.340
'Cause that's all I know,
in and out, back and forth.

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:29.309
I don't know nothin' else.

00:30:29.309 --> 00:30:30.142
(sirens blaring)

00:30:30.142 --> 00:30:32.963
Shit, if I did, I'd practice
somethin' different.

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:36.860
It's like the home training,
man, what I was taught is just

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:40.640
what I was taught, give a fuck
about the wrong information.

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:42.190
It's all they have to offer me.

00:30:43.323 --> 00:30:45.410
And I can live with that,
even with the mistakes, man.

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:48.010
I'm cool with that because
I knew sooner or later

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:49.880
there had to be a change come about.

00:30:49.880 --> 00:30:52.550
But I knew the change was gonna
have to come from within me.

00:30:52.550 --> 00:30:54.848
It started with my ass
and it was gonna with me.

00:30:54.848 --> 00:30:56.390
I don't give a fuck who
guided me, who misled me

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:58.123
along the way, that's irrelevant.

00:30:58.990 --> 00:31:01.243
Because I am responsible for me.

00:31:02.350 --> 00:31:04.360
I'm responsible for me.

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:06.500
I got to take care of me,
I got to look out for me,

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:09.403
do the best I can for me,
'cause that's all I got is me.

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:13.720
Hell, no, I don't do a
perfect job, I wish I did.

00:31:13.720 --> 00:31:16.380
But nothing beats a failure but a tribe.

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:18.000
And...

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:21.230
I hit those milestones,
you know, 50 years old,

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:24.195
finally got my driver's
license for the first time.

00:31:24.195 --> 00:31:25.816
And I've been home

00:31:25.816 --> 00:31:28.980
a little over three years,
that's a hell of a milestone.

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:30.870
You know, I think the
most I've ever stayed home

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:34.810
was maybe a little over two
years during my 19 trips.

00:31:34.810 --> 00:31:36.490
Almost two years, I was able to, almost

00:31:36.490 --> 00:31:39.485
able to stay home before
I had went right back.

00:31:39.485 --> 00:31:42.110
And the last time, like I said
before, was two days, man.

00:31:42.110 --> 00:31:44.850
So this is a milestone for
me, you know, I'm happy

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:46.443
where I'm at in my life.

00:31:48.280 --> 00:31:49.380
It's never easy

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:54.280
dealing with reality when
I been shucking and jiving

00:31:54.280 --> 00:31:57.210
and bobbin' and weavin' and
escaping it for so long,

00:31:57.210 --> 00:31:59.130
finally getting to the place
or the point in my life

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:00.980
where I'm really able to
deal with it for what it is.

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:04.240
See it for what it is, life,
my experience and everything

00:32:04.240 --> 00:32:06.390
that come about it,
that makes me who I am.

00:32:07.464 --> 00:32:08.297
And...

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:11.450
It wasn't easy

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:13.060
overcoming prison

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:15.900
because my first impression

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:18.100
was, that was the one that got me.

00:32:18.100 --> 00:32:18.933
That was the one that hooked me.

00:32:18.933 --> 00:32:20.670
Which was the Fire Camps, the
weights and all that stuff,

00:32:20.670 --> 00:32:23.350
the TV, the coffee and the
cigarettes and all that shit.

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:27.660
It was alluring, man, and it
was like a dream come true.

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:30.730
You know, and towards the end, the picture

00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:33.140
was, you know, it changed

00:32:33.140 --> 00:32:35.650
and when it changed in a prison settings,

00:32:35.650 --> 00:32:38.190
I just wasn't capable of
changing with it at the time.

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:39.940
So I had to go back and
forth, I had to endure

00:32:39.940 --> 00:32:42.490
some more shit, I had to do
it some more before my eyes

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:45.780
started to finally open to
see the reality of prison,

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:47.830
how it wasn't that no more,
it hasn't been like that

00:32:47.830 --> 00:32:52.420
in a long time but that was
what I was going on, that's what

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:55.140
motivated me, knowing
what it used to be like

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:58.530
but it had changed before
my very eyes and it took me

00:32:58.530 --> 00:33:00.810
a long time to see that change take place.

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:05.140
So anything that I do,
anything that I say,

00:33:05.140 --> 00:33:08.100
you know, hopefully, it
doesn't have to take as long.

00:33:08.100 --> 00:33:10.960
You know, but it seemed like
my experiences with life,

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:12.660
with drugs, with relationships,

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:14.563
with prison, with rehabs,

00:33:15.474 --> 00:33:17.130
man, it didn't take the first trip

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:19.840
or the umpteenth motherfuckin' trip, man.

00:33:19.840 --> 00:33:22.560
Even in my rehabs, I think
I've been to 30 programs

00:33:22.560 --> 00:33:25.660
prior to, you know, just
leading up to here, man.

00:33:25.660 --> 00:33:28.170
You know, 30 of the motherfuckers,
man, and I put my best

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:29.763
foot, I put my best foot.

00:33:30.630 --> 00:33:34.110
I put my best foot forward
in all those 30 vain attempts

00:33:34.110 --> 00:33:36.153
at trying to get my life
together from the crack, man.

00:33:36.153 --> 00:33:38.110
Now, that's a hell of a story
right there, if you want me

00:33:38.110 --> 00:33:40.950
to speak on that and it'd
be a pleasure to talk about

00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:43.200
the rehabs, man, 'cause I
wasn't bullshittin' when I went

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:45.810
to get that help, I really
wanted that help, I really wanted

00:33:45.810 --> 00:33:47.160
that change, I really
wanted to be that man

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:48.460
God created me to be.

00:33:48.460 --> 00:33:50.684
Just like when I went to
prison, I didn't want to go

00:33:50.684 --> 00:33:52.930
and I didn't want to leave
the same way I came in.

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:55.130
But I didn't know at the time, man,

00:33:55.130 --> 00:33:57.536
that what I put into it is
what I got up out of it.

00:33:57.536 --> 00:33:59.030
So I was just going through
the motions in the beginning

00:33:59.030 --> 00:34:00.640
'cause that's what everybody
was doing in prison,

00:34:00.640 --> 00:34:02.930
just going through the
motions, until they turn

00:34:02.930 --> 00:34:05.030
came to go home again.

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:07.400
And I was just in line waiting
for my motherfuckin' turn

00:34:07.400 --> 00:34:09.860
to go home but really didn't
change, really didn't put forth

00:34:09.860 --> 00:34:12.730
an effort and then I wonder
why I went right back

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:14.990
over and over and I wonder why I

00:34:14.990 --> 00:34:17.710
couldn't get up off that entanglement.

00:34:17.710 --> 00:34:20.057
I wonder why I said, "Man,
when it got too hard out here,

00:34:20.057 --> 00:34:22.748
"man, I don't give a fuck, man,
send my ass back to prison.

00:34:22.748 --> 00:34:23.581
"What took you so long?"

00:34:23.581 --> 00:34:24.803
'Cause I meant that.

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:30.240
Because what was abnormal
became normal for me.

00:34:30.240 --> 00:34:32.730
It became a part of my
lifestyle, part of my character

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:35.940
trait, prison, you know, I
didn't wear it as no badge

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:38.652
of honor or no bullshit
like that but it was just

00:34:38.652 --> 00:34:40.337
an intricate part of me.

00:34:40.337 --> 00:34:42.790
And it took me a long
time to see it for what

00:34:42.790 --> 00:34:45.423
it was and break those shackles.

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:47.850
You know?

00:34:47.850 --> 00:34:48.683
And...

00:34:49.623 --> 00:34:51.160
So I'm learning how to break
those shackles on the street,

00:34:51.160 --> 00:34:53.830
man, with my mind, man,
being in a prison in my mind

00:34:53.830 --> 00:34:55.460
out here on the streets, man.

00:34:55.460 --> 00:34:56.903
You know, I see it for
what it is out here,

00:34:56.903 --> 00:34:59.870
it's a beautiful day, I
love being free, I love

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:02.360
being home, I love putting
my best foot forward.

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:05.070
You know, put one foot in front
of the other, come what may.

00:35:05.070 --> 00:35:07.810
Yeah, I make my mistakes,
shit, I wish I was perfect

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:10.030
but I'm not perfect but I
do the motherfuckin' best

00:35:10.030 --> 00:35:12.930
I can with what I'm working
with and I can live with that.

00:35:14.285 --> 00:35:16.110
I don't know about nobody
else, I'm not no quitter.

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:19.600
I'm a fighter and I wanna be
more than just a survivor.

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:23.110
I want a life worth living,
man, and so far so good.

00:35:23.110 --> 00:35:25.740
I'm realizing that that dream,

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:29.310
one of those unspoken prayers,
I mean having my own place.

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:31.610
Now, I'm working on getting
transportation but there's some

00:35:31.610 --> 00:35:34.030
things I have to do in
order, you know, just to get,

00:35:34.030 --> 00:35:37.330
to get back to where I
would be more comfortable

00:35:37.330 --> 00:35:40.240
with being in this setting
because it's a struggle.

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:41.980
You know, it's a struggle for a brother

00:35:41.980 --> 00:35:44.190
who dropped out in the 10th grade,

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:46.120
who don't have no work
history, some more shit

00:35:46.120 --> 00:35:48.210
that's stacked up against,
it ain't easy comin' up from

00:35:48.210 --> 00:35:50.930
under all of that, plus you
add the prison setting on top

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:53.350
of that and the shit I learned
in there, man, it's almost

00:35:53.350 --> 00:35:56.100
impossible for a brother to
surface from up under all that

00:35:56.100 --> 00:35:57.823
bullshit but I managed to.

00:36:00.270 --> 00:36:01.920
So, I know I'm something special.

00:36:02.900 --> 00:36:05.076
I know I'm somebody to be reckoned with.

00:36:05.076 --> 00:36:06.720
I know I'm not your
average motherfuckin' Joe.

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:09.920
Thank God, 'cause I see
it for what it is in me.

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:12.200
I don't care how nobody
else see me or what they

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:14.230
take is on me, it don't matter.

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:17.350
It's what I think and care
about myself that matters today.

00:36:17.350 --> 00:36:19.951
And I love me and I'll do
everything in my power to make

00:36:19.951 --> 00:36:22.901
sure that I keep on putting
one foot in front of the other.

00:36:25.610 --> 00:36:26.633
I'm tired.

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:30.683
I'm sick and tired of
being sick and tired.

00:36:31.730 --> 00:36:33.180
So now it's all about action.

00:36:34.540 --> 00:36:37.487
Ready or not, prepared or
not, I'm going for what I know

00:36:37.487 --> 00:36:39.283
and that's broke, thank you.

